{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1109","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1109","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1109\",\"parent_revision\":\"7edfa476222c284c7cc5294bb1462954fbdf192fe5e5adfb9070dd28ef6b1e55\",\"root_revision\":\"7edfa476222c284c7cc5294bb1462954fbdf192fe5e5adfb9070dd28ef6b1e55\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: feat: Behavioral fingerprint tracking in PostToolUse hook\\nDate: 2026-04-10\\nGoal: Extend the PostToolUse hook to append JSONL telemetry records and add behavioral_summary.py to compute shift scores.\\nConditions:\\n1. Location of task_status_hook.py PostToolUse handler | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Glob confirmed plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py\\n2. How session_id is obtained in PostToolUse context | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-check \u2014 session_id = hook_input.get(\\\"session_id\\\", \\\"\\\") at line 864; flows from hook input to context file lookup\\n3. behavioral_summary.py does not yet exist \u2014 new file required | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Glob returned no match for behavioral_summary.py\\n4. compression-monitor Behavioral Footprint algorithm details | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-check VERIFIED research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md lines 76-86\\n5. Hook input JSON schema (fields available in PostToolUse event) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Fact-check confirmed hook accesses tool_name, tool_input, session_id, cwd, hook_event_name; timing data NOT available\\n6. Existing telemetry/ directory structure in dh_paths | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Impact-analyst confirmed telemetry/ absent from ensure_dirs() \u2014 dh_paths.py requires telemetry_dir() addition\\n7. Quality gates for new scripts in this directory | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: .claude/rules/linting-exceptions.md \u2014 scripts/** exempt from T201, S, DOC, ANN401\\n8. turn_number persistence mechanism across PostToolUse invocations (NEW) | Snapshot: \u2014 \u2192 Final: DERIVABLE | Basis: Impact-analyst identified gap; derivable as count-existing-JSONL-lines + 1 (no extra state required)\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Condition 5: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (fact-checker confirmed hook input schema; timing data claim refuted)\\n- Condition 6: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (impact-analyst confirmed telemetry/ absent from ensure_dirs)\\n- Condition 7: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (linting-exceptions.md documents scripts/** exceptions)\\n- Condition 8: NEW \u2014 DERIVABLE (turn_number derivable from JSONL line count; no MISSING block required)\\nAVAILABLE count: 7\\nDERIVABLE count: 1\\nMISSING count: 0\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-10)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**Score**: 8/10 \u2014 The PostToolUse hook fires on every Write, Edit, and Bash call, making this a hot-path integration point in every active session. The current implementation discards all behavioral signal silently: no log entry, no error, no observable indication that data was dropped. This silent discard makes context compression drift undetectable \u2014 the exact failure mode this feature exists to surface. The hook is already wired and firing; the missing telemetry path means every session is accumulating unobservable behavioral data. Deferring raises risk because the longer compression-induced drift goes undetected in production sessions, the harder post-hoc diagnosis becomes.\\\\n\\\\n**Effort**: Small-Medium (1-2 days) \u2014 2 file modifications, 1 new script, test coverage gap, 4 stale docs.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Developers running long sessions cannot detect when context compression has shifted the agent's tool-use patterns. The degradation is invisible until the agent produces obviously wrong output.\\n- Blocks: Any post-session analysis tooling that would consume behavioral data \u2014 none can be built until the telemetry records exist.\\n- Bottleneck: The development harness hook infrastructure is already deployed and firing on every tool call. The absence of telemetry output is the sole gap between \\\"hook fires\\\" and \\\"behavioral signal available\\\". Every session that runs without this feature produces a permanent data gap \u2014 behavioral history cannot be reconstructed retroactively.\\n- Affects: All agents and developers using the development-harness plugin in sessions with at least one Write, Edit, or Bash call \u2014 which is effectively every non-trivial session.\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Shift detection becomes possible: comparing first-half vs second-half tool-use patterns within a session surfaces compression-induced behavioral drift with a quantified score.\\n- Session quality analysis: JSONL records provide a timestamped audit trail of every tool invocation, enabling post-session review of response length trends, tool mix, and pacing.\\n- Debugging aid for compression events: when an agent behaves unexpectedly late in a session, the behavioral record gives a concrete artifact to examine rather than requiring session replay.\\n- Foundation for alerting: once per-session JSONL files exist, future tooling can read shift scores and emit warnings mid-session or at session boundaries.\\n- No new dependencies: the telemetry path uses only stdlib, keeping the hook fast and side-effect-free from a dependency standpoint.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen a session fires at least one PostToolUse event:\\n\\n- A JSONL file appears at `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl`.\\n- Each line in the file is a valid JSON object containing at minimum the keys `timestamp`, `tool_name`, `response_length`, and `turn_number`.\\n- The existing `LastActivity` timestamp behavior is unchanged \u2014 the timestamp file is still written on every PostToolUse event as before.\\n- Running `behavioral_summary.py --session {session_id}` produces a shift score comparing the first and second halves of the session's tool-use pattern.\\n- If a session fires no PostToolUse events, no JSONL file is created for that session (no empty files).\\n- The telemetry directory (`~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/`) is created automatically when the first record is written, without requiring manual setup.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. After a session that triggers at least one PostToolUse event, a file exists at `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl`.\\n2. Every line in that file parses as valid JSON (`python -c \\\"import json; [json.loads(l) for l in open(path)]\\\"` exits 0).\\n3. Every parsed record contains exactly the keys `timestamp`, `tool_name`, `response_length`, and `turn_number` (no required key absent, no required key null).\\n4. `behavioral_summary.py --session {session_id}` exits 0 and prints a numeric shift score to stdout.\\n5. The `last-activity` timestamp file is still updated on every PostToolUse event \u2014 confirmed by running a test session and verifying the file's mtime advances.\\n6. `dh_paths.py` exports a `telemetry_dir()` function that returns the telemetry path for a given project slug, and `ensure_dirs()` includes that path so the directory is created before first write.\\n7. Unit tests exist that exercise the JSONL append path in `task_status_hook.py` without mocking at the `main()` boundary \u2014 at minimum one test confirms a record is written and one confirms the record's keys.\\n8. An exception raised inside the telemetry append path (e.g., simulated disk full) does not cause the hook to exit non-zero \u2014 the LastActivity update still completes.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Hook implementation | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` |\\n| Path utilities | `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/dh_paths.py` |\\n| Research reference | `/home/user/claude_skills/research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md` \u2014 contains Behavioral Footprint pattern |\\n| Pattern | compression-monitor Behavioral Footprint pattern \u2014 source for JSONL record schema and shift score computation approach |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: `dh_paths.py` must expose `telemetry_dir()` and include it in `ensure_dirs()` before the JSONL write logic in `task_status_hook.py` can be added \u2014 if the directory is not guaranteed to exist, the first write will raise `FileNotFoundError`. This is an internal ordering dependency within the same changeset, not a separate backlog item.\\n- Depends on: No external package dependencies \u2014 all new code must use Python stdlib only (json, pathlib, datetime). No `uv add` required.\\n- Blocks: Any future alerting or mid-session shift detection tooling that would consume the JSONL files.\\n- Blocks: Any documentation updates describing PostToolUse behavior \u2014 those updates cannot be accurate until the feature ships.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall-Medium (1-2 days) \u2014 scope is bounded: 2 file modifications (`task_status_hook.py`, `dh_paths.py`), 1 new script (`behavioral_summary.py`), test coverage for the new append path, and 4 stale documentation updates. The hook integration point is already wired; the new code adds a JSONL append call with error isolation. The companion script is a fresh file with no existing behavior to preserve. The main uncertainty is the `turn_number` persistence design decision (see Concerns), which could add a small amount of scope if option (b) \u2014 persisting the counter in the context file \u2014 is chosen over the simpler line-count approach.\\n\\n### Concerns\\n\\n- `turn_number` persistence is an unresolved design decision that blocks planning. Three options exist: (a) count existing lines in the JSONL file and add 1 \u2014 simple, requires no extra state; (b) persist a counter in the context file (`active-task-{session_id}.json`) \u2014 adds a read/write dependency on an existing file; (c) count lines on each open (equivalent to a but explicit). The choice affects how the hook interacts with the context file and whether `turn_number` is monotonic across partial session restarts. This must be decided before implementation begins.\\n\\n- Hot-path risk: the telemetry append runs on every Write, Edit, and Bash call. Any unhandled exception in the append path will cause the hook to exit non-zero, which surfaces as a tool-call failure to the agent. The append path must be wrapped in error isolation (catch, log to stderr, continue) so that a disk full, permission error, or JSON serialization failure never propagates to the hook's exit code. This is a correctness requirement, not a nice-to-have \u2014 it must be verified by Acceptance Criterion 8.\\n\\n- Timing data: the item description mentions `latency` as a discarded signal, but fact-checking confirmed the hook input JSON does not contain timing data. `latency` cannot be captured from hook input alone. This field should not appear in the JSONL schema unless a timing source is identified during planning.\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: NOT_APPLICABLE\\n**Rationale**: This is a new additive feature. There is no existing implementation of behavioral fingerprint tracking in PostToolUse hook to compare against design intent. The alignment-analyst agent was not included in the NARROW scope swarm because there is no prior implementation to assess for drift.\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nModified:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` \u2014 PostToolUse handler; needs JSONL append logic\\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_paths.py` \u2014 must add `telemetry_dir()` and include it in `ensure_dirs()`\\n\\nCreated:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/behavioral_summary.py` \u2014 new companion script; computes per-window fingerprints and shift score\\n\\nDocumentation (stale, needs update):\\n- 4 files describe PostToolUse as writing only `last-activity` \u2014 these must be updated to reflect the new telemetry output. Exact paths to be identified during planning.\\n\\nTest files:\\n- Existing test file(s) for `task_status_hook.py` \u2014 new test cases covering the JSONL append path required.\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: missing-guardrail\\n**Rationale**: The PostToolUse hook fires on every Write, Edit, and Bash call and correctly updates LastActivity. However, it allows all behavioral signal (tool name, response length, turn count) to pass through without collection \u2014 meaning compression-induced behavioral drift proceeds through the session without any detection gate. The system infrastructure exists (hook fires, session_id available, dh_paths provides state root) but no gate exists to collect the signal that would expose silent behavioral shifts.\\n**Analysis Method**: Structural gap analysis \u2014 hook fires correctly but produces no telemetry output as a structural omission, not a logic error.\\n**Scenario Target**: Long-running session with context compression event \u2192 behavioral drift goes undetected \u2192 no artifact for post-session analysis.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nN/A \u2014 this is a new additive feature, not a defect. There is no failure condition to reproduce prior to implementation. Measurable signal is defined in the item: JSONL file exists after session with PostToolUse events, each line valid JSON with required keys.\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`task_status_hook.py` fires on every PostToolUse event (Write|Edit|Bash) but only updates a `LastActivity` timestamp. Tool name, response length, tool-call count per turn, and latency are all discarded. The hook input JSON contains `tool_name`, `tool_input`, and timing data but none of it is persisted.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md -- pattern: Behavioral Footprint tool-use pattern tracking\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-29\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n<!-- artifact-gist:86490d47f4759030a770be986c8c0cd5 -->\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe PostToolUse handler appends a lightweight behavioral record to a JSONL file at `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl`. Each record contains: `timestamp`, `tool_name`, `response_length` (chars), `turn_number` (incrementing counter per session). A companion `behavioral_summary.py` script reads the JSONL and computes per-window fingerprints (mean response length, tool-call ratio, tool diversity) to detect shifts across the session.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nFile `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl` exists after a session with at least one PostToolUse event. Each line is valid JSON with keys `timestamp`, `tool_name`, `response_length`, `turn_number`. Running `behavioral_summary.py --session {session_id}` outputs a shift score comparing the first and second halves of the session.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **behavioral fingerprint tracking in posttooluse hook** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Fact-Check Results (2026-04-10)\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Claim 1: task_status_hook.py fires on every PostToolUse event (Write|Edit|Bash)\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n- Source: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py` lines 929-933\\n- Evidence: `if event_name == \\\"PostToolUse\\\": tool_name = hook_input.get(\\\"tool_name\\\", \\\"\\\"); if tool_name in {\\\"Write\\\", \\\"Edit\\\", \\\"Bash\\\"}: handle_activity_update(hook_input)`\\n\\n### Claim 2: Hook ONLY updates LastActivity timestamp for PostToolUse events\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n- Source: Same file, lines 853-903 (handle_activity_update function)\\n- Evidence: `sam_update_plan_fields(full_path, task_id, set_fields={\\\"last-activity\\\": timestamp})` \u2014 only the `last-activity` field is set\\n\\n### Claim 3: Hook input JSON contains tool_name, tool_input, and timing data\\n**VERDICT: PARTIALLY REFUTED**\\n- Source: Same file + `/home/user/claude_skills/.claude/plan/plugin-creator-hooks/claude-hooks-doc.md`\\n- Evidence: Hook accesses `tool_name` (line 931) and `session_id` (line 864), but NO timing data is extracted or used\\n- The hook input DOES contain `tool_name` and `tool_input` per Claude Code hook schema, but timing data is not referenced anywhere in the handler code\\n\\n### Claim 4: session_id obtained from context file at ~/.dh/projects/{slug}/context/active-task-{session_id}.json\\n**VERDICT: PARTIALLY REFUTED**\\n- Source: Same file, lines 297-312, 315-338\\n- Evidence: The path construction is correct (line 312), BUT:\\n  - `session_id` is extracted FROM the hook input (line 864: `session_id = hook_input.get(\\\"session_id\\\", \\\"\\\")`)\\n  - The context file is READ using session_id as a key to retrieve task information\\n  - The claim reverses the dependency: session_id \u2192 context file, not the other way around\\n\\n### Claim 5: research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md describes \\\"Behavioral Footprint\\\" pattern\\n**VERDICT: VERIFIED**\\n- Source: `/home/user/claude_skills/research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md` lines 76-86\\n- Evidence: Section titled \\\"Behavioral Footprint \u2014 Tool-Use Pattern Tracking\\\" with implementation details in `behavioral_footprint.py`\\n- Note: The pattern is for POST-compression analysis of behavioral shifts, not for collecting real-time behavioral data during tool use\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_status_hook.py::handle_activity_update` - This function is the sole PostToolUse handler being extended. It currently writes only `last-activity` timestamp via `sam_update_plan_fields`. The item adds a second write path: appending a JSONL record to `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl`. The function signature and hook entry point remain the same, but the body gains file I/O, `response_length` extraction from hook input, and `turn_number` tracking (requires a per-session counter that persists across invocations). | Risk: HIGH | Why: The hook fires on every Write/Edit/Bash event \u2014 any latency regression or unhandled exception in the new JSONL write path would degrade every tool call in every session. The 10-second timeout in `hooks.json` leaves headroom but JSONL open/append on a hot path requires care.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/behavioral_summary.py` - New file to create. Reads JSONL telemetry files from `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/`, computes per-window fingerprints (mean response length, tool-call ratio, tool diversity), and outputs a shift score. Does not exist yet. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: New file \u2014 no regression risk, but must conform to PEP 723 script conventions, linting rules, and the `uv run --script` invocation pattern enforced in this repo.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_paths.py` - Defines every per-project state directory via `state_root()`. The new `telemetry/` directory is not declared here. The item requires `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/telemetry/` to exist before the hook can append to it. `ensure_dirs()` currently creates `backlog/`, `plan/`, `milestones/`, `research/`, `context/`, and `reports/` \u2014 it does not create `telemetry/`. Adding a `telemetry_dir()` function and including it in `ensure_dirs()` is needed, or the hook must call `mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)` inline. | Risk: HIGH | Why: If `telemetry/` does not exist at hook time, `open(..., \\\"a\\\")` will raise `FileNotFoundError`, the hook exits with code 2, and Claude Code surfaces an error on every Write/Edit/Bash call.\\n\\n### Code - Consumers\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts/task_format.py` - Imports from `task_status_hook` are not present (confirmed via grep), but is co-located in the same `scripts/` directory. No direct consumer relationship. | Risk: LOW | Why: Peer file with no import relationship; unaffected by the change.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/kage-bunshin/scripts/health.py`, `monitor.py`, `spawn.py` - These scripts do not import from `task_status_hook.py`. They operate over subprocess PIDs, not hook output. However `monitor.py` and `health.py` observe session state; behavioral telemetry files are a new session artifact they do not read. No direct breakage, but downstream observability tooling may eventually want to incorporate the new telemetry. | Risk: LOW | Why: No import or runtime dependency. Noted for future integration opportunity.\\n\\n### Code - Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_paths.py::ensure_dirs` - `ensure_dirs()` creates the known per-project directory tree. Without adding `telemetry_dir()` to this function's loop, the directory will be absent for fresh project states. The hook must either call `mkdir` inline or rely on `ensure_dirs` being called first. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Structural dependency \u2014 telemetry writes will fail on a fresh DH state root unless the directory exists.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/dh_paths.py::state_root` - `state_root()` is the anchor for all path functions. A new `telemetry_dir()` function must follow the existing pattern: `return state_root(project_root) / \\\"telemetry\\\"`. This is additive, not breaking. | Risk: LOW | Why: Additive extension \u2014 no existing callers change.\\n\\n### Tests\\n\\n- `tests/test_task_status_hook_profiles.py` - Tests `handle_activity_update` by mocking the function entirely in `main()` integration tests. Does not exercise the body of `handle_activity_update`. The new JSONL write inside `handle_activity_update` is untested by existing tests \u2014 mocking the function at the `main()` level means the new logic is invisible to current test coverage. New unit tests are needed for: JSONL record format, telemetry directory creation, turn_number increment, `response_length` extraction from hook input, and graceful failure when JSONL write fails. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Zero existing test coverage for `handle_activity_update` body \u2014 the new hot-path write goes entirely unverified.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/tests/test_task_status_hook/test_github_sync.py` - Tests `handle_activity_update` only via the assertion that it does NOT call GitHub code. This is a guard test \u2014 confirms separation of concerns. The JSONL write is outside the scope of this guard, so the test remains valid but does not verify the new path. | Risk: LOW | Why: Guard test remains correct; no coverage gap introduced here.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/tests/test_task_status_hook/test_subagent_stop_integration.py` - Tests SubagentStop end-to-end; does not touch `handle_activity_update`. Unaffected. | Risk: LOW | Why: Different handler path.\\n\\n- No test exists for `behavioral_summary.py` \u2014 must be created alongside the script. Tests must cover: JSONL parse, window fingerprint computation, shift score calculation, empty-file handling, malformed-line resilience. | Risk: HIGH | Why: New untested module that produces a shift score \u2014 incorrect math or off-by-one windowing would silently produce wrong output.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/workflow-architecture-diagram.md` (section 6.2, table \u00a72.6, producer-consumer table) - Three specific locations describe PostToolUse behavior: (1) Section 6.2 \\\"PostToolUse (Write|Edit|Bash)\\\" step 5 lists only `last-activity` as the field written. It will become stale \u2014 the handler now also appends to the JSONL telemetry file, and step 5 text must be updated. (2) The producer-consumer table row `last-activity field in task | task_status_hook.py PostToolUse handler | progress reporting` must gain a second row for `behavioral-{session_id}.jsonl`. (3) The \u00a72.6 context file description note \\\"Read by `task_status_hook.py` PostToolUse handler\\\" is still accurate but incomplete \u2014 the handler now also writes telemetry. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: The diagram is authoritative for contributors; stale step-5 text will mislead developers implementing related features.\\n\\n- `.claude/docs/workflow-architecture-diagram.md` - A `.claude/` mirror of the above document with identical PostToolUse section 6.2 content. Same staleness risk as the canonical version. Both must be updated together. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Two-document drift problem \u2014 the mirror diverges from the canonical source if only one is updated.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` (section \\\"Authorized Writers\\\" and \\\"Hook Runtime Environment Variables\\\") - Two claims become incomplete: (1) \\\"Fields written: `last-activity: <ISO timestamp>`\\\" (only lists one output). (2) The `CLAUDE_SKILLS_HOOK_PROFILE=minimal` description says \\\"PostToolUse handler is skipped (no LastActivity updates)\\\" \u2014 if minimal also skips the JSONL write, this description remains accurate; if telemetry is emitted even under minimal, the description must be updated. This is a design decision that must be made explicit. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Authorized-writers section is relied upon to know who writes which fields; adding a new output (the JSONL file) without updating the list creates an undocumented side effect.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/SKILL.md` (Hook Integration section, Timestamp Field Responsibilities table) - The table row \\\"PostToolUse (Activity Tracking)\\\" describes only `LastActivity` timestamp updates. It will become stale and must include the new JSONL telemetry output and describe `behavioral_summary.py` usage. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: Skill files are read by AI agents and developers to understand hook behavior \u2014 stale descriptions will cause incorrect assumptions.\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/start-task/SKILL.md` - Declares `PostToolUse: Write|Edit|Bash` hooks pointing to `task_status_hook.py`. Does not describe what the hook does. No content staleness, but agents reading this file learn nothing about the new behavioral telemetry. Low risk \u2014 the hook registration entry needs no update but may benefit from a one-line description note. | Risk: LOW | Why: Hook registration remains valid; only the description is absent.\\n\\n- `research/ai-observability/compression-monitor.md` - Source research for the behavioral fingerprint algorithm. Describes `behavioral_footprint.py` fields: `response_length`, `tool_call_frequency`, `latency`. The item adopts a subset of these fields (`response_length`, `tool_name`, `turn_number`, `timestamp`). The research doc is informational, not operational \u2014 no update required. | Risk: LOW | Why: Read-only reference material; not operational documentation.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/hooks/hooks.json` (PostToolUse entry, timeout) - The PostToolUse hook currently has a 10-second timeout. Adding JSONL file I/O (open \u2192 append \u2192 close) on the hot path typically adds 1-5 ms per event. For sessions with high tool-call density, the cumulative timeout risk is negligible. However, if the telemetry directory does not exist and `mkdir` is called inline, that adds a stat() + mkdir() syscall overhead. The matcher `Write|Edit|Bash` and command path remain unchanged. | Risk: LOW | Why: Timeout is generous for the operation; no config change needed unless telemetry path resolution adds unexpected latency.\\n\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` (test-python job) - Runs `uv run -q pytest` against `testpaths` defined in `pyproject.toml`. New tests for `handle_activity_update` body and `behavioral_summary.py` will be picked up automatically. No CI configuration change required. The `typecheck-ty` and `typecheck-python` jobs will type-check the new `behavioral_summary.py` \u2014 it must carry proper type annotations and PEP 723 inline metadata. | Risk: LOW | Why: CI structure already covers new Python files; no workflow modification needed. New tests expand coverage automatically.\\n\\n- `pyproject.toml` (`pythonpath` list) - Currently adds `./plugins/development-harness/skills/implementation-manager/scripts` and the test subdirectory to `pythonpath`. If `behavioral_summary.py` has its own tests under a new `tests/test_behavioral_summary/` directory, that path must be added to `pythonpath` or `testpaths` to be discovered. | Risk: LOW | Why: Minor additive change if tests live in a new subdirectory; existing test discovery already covers the scripts directory.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` - Contains the note \\\"Hook behavior on SubagentStop: When a sub-agent finishes, `task_status_hook.py` marks the task complete in the local task file.\\\" Agents reading this learn what the hook does on SubagentStop but nothing about PostToolUse behavioral telemetry. No instruction becomes wrong, but the description is incomplete after the item ships. | Risk: LOW | Why: SubagentStop description remains accurate; PostToolUse is not 